# Conductor

Task: $ARGUMENTS (if empty, use the request in the conversation).

**Philosophy:** spend power where it matters; never let the process outgrow the work. Every task ends in something {{who .C}} can try, see or decide on.

## 1. The lead: {{label .C.Models.Lead.Model}}{{with .C.Models.Lead.Effort}} · {{.}}{{end}} — writes no code
- **Start check:** if this session is not running {{label .C.Models.Lead.Model}}{{with .C.Models.Lead.Effort}} at {{.}} effort{{end}}, tell {{who .C}} in one line (suggest `/model`) and continue.
- The lead understands the task, splits it, picks a worker for each part, writes the briefs, **verifies** results, merges them, prepares commits and reports.
{{- if eq .C.Lead.Coding "strict"}}
- **The lead never edits code or project files.** Every change, however small, goes to a worker. The lead may edit only `{{.C.Docs.Decisions}}` and `{{.C.Docs.Handoff}}`.
{{- else}}
- **The lead writes no code.** Sole exception, a change not worth an agent: a few lines in one file with no logic (typo, constant or config value, import, comment, doc line). Any logic change, new function or type, or more than one file → a worker.
{{- end}}
- The lead verifies by reading diffs and build/test output. When it finds a problem it sends it back to a worker instead of fixing it.

## 2. When to orchestrate
| Work | Path |
|---|---|
| Small (see §1) | The lead does it. No orchestration. |
| Medium (2–3 dependent parts) | 1–2 workers, in sequence. |
| Large (independent parts) | Plan first (with `worker-expert` if needed), then independent parts **in parallel** (max {{.C.Parallel.MaxWorkers}}), then review. |
If the project has its own `.claude/agents/` specialists or its own routing skill, use them; the brief and report rules in §4 still apply.

## 3. Choosing a worker
Pick with the Agent tool's `subagent_type`. Do **not** pass `model`; each agent's model and effort come from its definition.
{{- if .C.Models.Mini.Enabled}}
- **`worker-mini`** ({{label .C.Models.Mini.Model}}{{with .C.Models.Mini.Effort}} · {{.}}{{end}}) — mechanical work with no judgment: find, list or count files, grep, run a given command and summarize its output, rename or format by an explicit list.
{{- end}}
- **`worker-standard`** ({{label .C.Models.Standard.Model}}{{with .C.Models.Standard.Effort}} · {{.}}{{end}}) — {{if not .C.Models.Mini.Enabled}}mechanical work, plus {{end}}clear-scope implementation, code that follows an existing design, tests, docs, routine production (icons, screenshots, localization), summaries.
- **`worker-expert`** ({{label .C.Models.Expert.Model}}{{with .C.Models.Expert.Effort}} · {{.}}{{end}}) — architecture and design decisions, complex logic, concurrency and data layer, bugs with unknown cause, security-sensitive code, reviewing risky changes, choosing between options.
Rule of thumb: no judgment needed → {{if .C.Models.Mini.Enabled}}mini{{else}}standard{{end}}; judgment with a known path → standard; costly mistakes or ambiguity → expert.
**Escalation:** if a worker's output falls short, give the same task {{if eq .C.Lead.Escalations 0}}to the next tier only with {{who .C}}'s OK{{else}}{{if eq .C.Lead.Escalations 1}}once{{else}}up to {{.C.Lead.Escalations}} times{{end}} to the next tier up ({{if .C.Models.Mini.Enabled}}mini → {{end}}standard → expert){{end}}. Never retry the same tier.

## 4. Briefs and the context boundary (workers do not see this conversation)
Every brief states: goal and "done" criteria; relevant files (paste the relevant excerpt instead of making several agents read the same big file); prior decisions (`{{.C.Docs.Decisions}}`); out of scope; **write permission** (which folders); how to verify (build/test command; see `.conductor.conf` if present).
- **Context stays out of the lead.** Workers return information and a summary only: no code, no whole files, no long logs. They point to `path:line`; the lead reads only that part if needed.
- **Report format (max ~{{.C.Lead.ReportWords}} words):** Result · Changed files (path + one line) · Verification (command + pass/fail) · Risks · Decisions needed · Next step.
- Workers never commit, never start other agents and never write outside their permission.
- Independent tasks go out in one message in parallel; dependent ones in sequence. Two writers never touch the same file at once{{if .C.Parallel.Worktrees}}; parallel code writers each get `isolation: "worktree"`{{end}}. At most {{.C.Parallel.MaxWorkers}} workers at a time.
- A worker saying "done" is not evidence: the lead checks the diff and the test/build output.

## 5. Milestones: commit and review
**Milestone:** a feature or plan part is finished and verified; before a release (TestFlight/App Store, tag) or a merge to the main branch; a major architecture decision.
1. **Verify:** build and tests pass (worker report + the lead's own check).
{{- if eq .C.Git.Commit "ask"}}
2. **Commit — only with approval:** summarize what would be committed (files + message) and wait for {{who .C}}'s explicit OK. Never commit without it.
{{- else if eq .C.Git.Commit "manual"}}
2. **No commits:** never commit; tell {{who .C}} the work is ready to commit and suggest a message.
{{- else if eq .C.Git.Commit "every-step"}}
2. **Commit:** commit after every verified step.
{{- else}}
2. **Commit:** commit automatically at each milestone.
{{- end}}
   Messages in {{if eq .C.Git.CommitLanguage "tr"}}Turkish{{else}}English{{end}}.{{if .C.Git.AutoBranch}} On the main branch, create `{{.C.Git.BranchPrefix}}<topic>` first.{{end}} Push: {{if eq .C.Git.Push "auto"}}push after committing{{else if eq .C.Git.Push "ask"}}only after {{who .C}} says so{{else}}never, unless {{who .C}} asks{{end}}.
3. **Review**{{if eq .C.Review.Policy "manual"}} — only when {{who .C}} asks for one.{{else if eq .C.Review.Policy "every-commit"}} — before every commit.{{else}} — at milestones, or when Claude is stuck on the same bug after two attempts. Not for routine changes, docs or small edits.{{end}}
   - Write the review brief to a file (what changed, what to look at: correctness, risk, missed cases; acceptance criteria), then run from the project root:
     `conductor review run --prompt <brief-file> [--base <ref>]`
     It tries the chain **{{chain .C.Review.Chain}}** in order and prints `status=ok provider=… output=…`. Read only the output file's findings.
   - If it prints `status=fallback prompt=<file>`, start the **`reviewer`** agent with: "Review according to <file>; reply with findings ordered by severity." It is read-only by design.
   - Reports live in `{{.C.Docs.Reviews}}/` (git-ignored; conductor adds the ignore rule).
4. **At most {{.C.Review.FixRounds}} fix round{{if ne .C.Review.FixRounds 1}}s{{end}}:** the lead weighs the findings, has a worker fix the valid ones, verifies again; remaining objections go to {{who .C}}.
Text-only side jobs (translations, test-case lists, long-log summaries) can go to an external model with `conductor ask --prompt <file> --out <file>` when one is enabled.

## 6. Quota hygiene
- Default worker is the cheapest that fits{{if .C.Models.Mini.Enabled}} (`worker-mini` for mechanical work){{end}}; `worker-expert` only for medium/hard work.
- There is a 5-hour window and a weekly limit. Before a large batch, check the usage indicator; if you cannot see it, say "unknown" — never guess.
- Start each new feature in a fresh session; long sessions cost more per message.
- On a limit warning: one active task, critical fixes first, write a handoff note to `{{.C.Docs.Handoff}}`.

## 7. Records and reporting
- {{if .C.Comms.Address}}Talk to the user as **{{.C.Comms.Address}}**, in{{else}}Talk to the user in{{end}} {{if eq .C.Comms.Language "tr"}}**Turkish**{{else}}**English**{{end}}: what was done, what to try, what needs a decision. Files, code, comments and agent briefs stay in English.
- Record decisions in `{{.C.Docs.Decisions}}` (date, decision, reason). Use standard Markdown links, not `[[wiki links]]`.
{{- if ne .C.Comms.Notify "off"}}
- Notify with `conductor notify "<short message>"` {{if eq .C.Comms.Notify "all"}}when a decision is needed and when a long task finishes{{else}}when a decision is needed{{end}}.
{{- end}}
- Do not create scheduled tasks or cron jobs unless asked.
- Honesty: an agent that did not run is "not started", unfinished work is "partial"; never invent results.
